<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Campine</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kempen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Campine, or Kempen, is a region of sandy heathland and pine forest in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, roughly between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> and the Demer. In late Roman times it was part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toxandria">Toxandria</a>, where the Emperor Julian allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salian-franks">Salian Franks</a> to settle in 358. Poor soils kept it thinly settled until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mining and industry developed in the 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/campine.jpg" alt="Campine" width="320" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mystro82 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DeKempen_NL_BE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Campine takes in a large northern and eastern part of Antwerp Province and neighbouring parts of Limburg and the far north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flemish-brabant">Flemish Brabant</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-brabant">North Brabant</a> south-west of Eindhoven and Dutch Limburg around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weert">Weert</a>. The river Demer forms its southern edge, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> valley its eastern one, and to the east it runs on into the related Groote Peel. Beneath it lies the Campine Basin, Devonian and Carboniferous rocks on the northern flank of the Brabant Massif. The Medieval Latin name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>, first recorded in the mid-11th century by Stepelinus, a monk of Saint-Trond, comes from a root meaning field and denotes uncultivated land.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Its poor sandy soil left few old or large towns, and most of them, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasselt">Hasselt</a>, Diest, Lier, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a>, Tilburg and Eindhoven, stand at its rim; Turnhout, with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> trade, and Herentals, a textile centre whose cloth hall survives, are exceptions. The quiet of the thinly peopled heath drew monks to the abbeys of Achel, Postel, Westmalle and Tongerlo. The first pines were sown in the Gierlebos at Vosselaar in 1667 for Amalia van Solms, and pine later covered large areas to supply pit props for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mines.</p><p>Industry arrived from the later 19th century: silica sand was quarried at Mol from 1872, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philips">Philips</a> was founded in Eindhoven in 1891, and metalworks grew at Balen, Overpelt and Lommel. The Campine coal basin was worked above all after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, bringing new industry to Geel, Beringen and Genk, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>'s first nuclear installation opened at Mol in 1962 and military bases were placed on the open land. Writers such as Hendrik Conscience and Georges Eekhoud set many books there, and painters such as Jakob Smits painted its landscapes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Parts of the old heath, marsh and forest survive in reserves such as the Kalmthoutse Heide and the Hoge Kempen National Park near Genk, opened in March 2006 and placed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s Tentative List in May 2011. The open-air museum of Bokrijk keeps much of the region's rural heritage, including its long-facade farms.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Campine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They expanded north into the <b>Kempen</b> heathlands, and the county became one of the middle-ranking principalities of Lower Lorraine.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-loon">County of Loon</a></li><li><blockquote>In 358 the Caesar Julian forced the submission of those who had settled in Toxandria, in the modern <b>Kempen</b>, and let them remain as allies of the Roman Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/salian-franks">Salian Franks</a></li><li><blockquote>Known chiefly from Pliny, who lists them among the peoples of Gallia Belgica, the Texuandri lived in the region between the Meuse and the Scheldt, in the modern <b>Belgian Kempen</b> and Dutch North Brabant.</blockquote><a href="/state/texuandri">Texuandri</a></li></ul></section>
